#01  Meet Stephen
===============================================================================================

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_Butterworth

  In 1930, Stephen Butterworth asked himself a naïve question: what would the simplest low-pass filter be?
  He didn't know yet that "simple" would mean "perfect".
  Nor could he know that his name was to become at least one mandatory chapter in every filter theory.

  Source: I travelled through time and read his mind.
  I'm trying to be poetic. Would you rather have me quote Aristotle again?

  Actual source:
    "On the Theory of Filter Amplifiers", S. Butterworth
    (in "Experimental Wireless & the Wireless Engineer" n° 85, October 1930)
    https://archive.org/details/sim_electronic-technology_experimental-wireless-and-the-wireless-engineer_1930-10_7_85/page/536/mode/2up

  Stephen was so badass that he didn't even use the Laplace transform.
  This wasn't necessarily an act of rebellion: there was no standard method yet.
  No social norm in filter design. Complete creative Freedom.

  There were no digital filters in the late 1920's. Back then this was pure electronics.
  The problem was to be understood as "design a circuit".
  The 6-page article is pretty short if you reduce it to its math part.

  I don't know that he used the term "transfer function" -- but he wrote "ratio of the output [...] to the input [...]".
  Nevertheless his method suggests that he knew that the amplitude response of a filter of order N is the absolute value
  of a ratio complex polynomials whose denominator is of order N.

  He defined f0 the cutoff frequency and any frequency f as f = x f0.
  So  x  =  f / f0. That's our ω / ωc, so we're in unit-cutoff notation. So our numerator is 1 (LP, remember?).

  Then he defines F the "filter factor". He's focusing on amplitudes so that's our |H (jω)|.
  (Or H (jω') if you accept my rejection of standard unit-cutoff notation.)

  So our denominator is the square root of a polynomial of order 2N (he used m for N).

  What's the simplest polynomial of order μ? I'd say:  x^μ.

  Ok, so the simplest amplitude response for a LP filter of order N is:
     _______
    ⎟    1   
    ⎟ ------
    ⎷  x^2N
  
  Except this is not a LP... We want the amplitude to be 1 when x = 0, and 0 when x = ∞.
  We already have the second condition.
  Now:  1  =  √(1)  =  √(1 / 1)  =  √( 1 / (1 + 0) )  =  √( 1 / (1 + 0^2N) )

  Ok so just add 1 to the numerator and we're good.

  Stephen writes:

    F  =  (1 + x^2m) ^ (1/2)

  which translates as:                                              _________________         ___________________
                                                                   ⎟       ωc^2N            ⎟          1
    |H (jω)|^-2  =  1 + (ω/ωc)^2N    which means:    |H (jω)|  =   ⎟ ----------------   =   ⎟ ------------------  =  F
                                                                   ⎷  ωc^2N  +  ω^2N        ⎷   1  +  (ω/ωc)^2N

  /*
    Notice that (assuming x is never negative):

      #  If N = 1, we retrieve the amplitude response of a 1-pole RC low-pass. We already have one solution.

      #  If x = 1 (that is ω = ωc),  F  =  (1 + 1)^(1/2)  =  1 / √(2)
           so any order will have the same amplitude at the cutoff frequency as a 1-pole RC low-pass.

      #  If x < 1 (that is ω < ωc), x^2N tends to zero when N tends to infinity. Then 1 + x^2N tends to 1,
           and F tends to  1 / √(1)  =  1.
           I.e. the pass-band tends to ideal. Indeed, any frequency under the cutoff has a gain of 1, so its amplitude is untouched.

      #  If x > 1 (that is ω > ωc), x^2N tends to infinity when N tends to infinity. Then 1 + x^2N tends to infinity,
           and F tends to  1 / √(∞)  =  0.
           I.e. the stop-band tends to ideal. Indeed, any frequency over the cutoff has a gain of 0, so its attenuation is total.

      #  Infinite N means ideal pass-band, ideal stop-band and no transition-band.
           This looks like the formula for the engineer's ideal filter.

           //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low-pass_filter#Ideal_and_real_filters

           Although it's a bit paradoxical since it means a gain at cutoff of  1 / √(2)  and yet no transition-band...
           But paradoxes tend to show up when you use infinity as a number.

           Still: the transition-band is as infinitesimal, the pass-band is as immaculate, the stop-band is as steep --
           -- as the order is high.
  */

  Remember:  |z| = 0    <=>   z = 0    so the poles of the transfer function are poles of the amplitude response.
  Ok but the only solution to  √(x) = 0  is  x = 0.
  So the poles of |H (jω)| are the roots of |H (jω)|^-2 which, if we keep Stephen's  x = ω/ωc  gives  |H (jω)|^-2  =  1 + x^2N.
  Indeed the exponent -2 is 2 times -1, where "2" means "square" or "ignore the square root",
  and "-1" means "inverse" or "ignore the numerator".

  So we want the solutions to  ƒ (x)  =  1 + x^2N  =  0.
  But wait. That's a 2Nth-order polynomial: it has 2N roots. Our filter is of order N: it has N poles.
  We'll get twice too many solutions... Right. Good.

  When studying polynomials, we've seen that:

    (1)  any polynomial can be expressed in terms of its roots;
    (2)  any polynomial of order N is a product of N polynomials of order 1 (affine).    //  (where N is a positive integer)
  
  Which means that any set of half the roots of ƒ (x) can be used to build the denominator of an N-pole filter's transfer function.
  So if we don't like some root, good news: we need to drop half of them anyway.
  E.g. if we want a stable filter we'll drop any r_n with  Re (r_n) > 0.
  Just hope at least half of the roots have a negative real part then.
  /*
    Of course if we want the ultimate unstable filter we could do the opposite.
    Dark-energy filter: "Oh, so you have a stop-band? Cute. I have a black hole."
    Too late, I've just coined the name.
    What's the book's license again?
  */

  Ok so:  ƒ (x)  =  1 + x^2N  =  0.

  Butterworth didn't solve the equation himself: he writes "see Todhunter's Trigonometry, p. 307".
  The only work by Isaac Todhunter I could access online is called "Spherical Trigonometry (for the Use of Colleges and Schools)".
  It has 171 pages.
  I guess we're on our own now.

  //  Traditions vs. logic

  We've reached the point where we'll go beyond Stephen Butterworth's article.
  What he calls a "single element" is already a second-order filter.
  So his "m", our "N", is always even.

  People after him found a way to apply his model to odd orders, but with a distinct formula,
  which is neither convenient nor elegant.

  This also comes from the fact that Stephen used a ready-to-serve formula provided by Isaac Todhunter.
  The math part is a small fraction of his article: he's an engineer occupied with electronics.
  Almost as if I should have named this chapter "Isaac Todhunter"... But who would read that?

  Let's grant ourselves an extra level of Freedom and assume N can be odd right from the start.
  We should end up with a single formula for even and odd orders.

