  Solution:
    First of all: not necessarily:  x - j  =  0  has one root:  j  -- purely imaginary.
    But the statement about x^2N being a product of x stands.
    For instance, if N = 1, then  x^2N  =  -1  means  x² = -1  and  x = ±j... Almost unstable.

    But  x =  s'/j  so if  x = ±j  then  s' = ±1. And we'll pick -1 for the stake of stability.

  Anyway. Apart from the possible extra real root, our results will always come in complex conjugate pairs.
  Then we can build second-order blocks as products of conjugates.

  We're dealing with complex numbers and exponentiation. The exponential form should make our lives easier.

    -1  =  -1  +  0 j    so   Re (-1)  =  -1    and  Im (-1)  =  0

  Then:

    |-1|  =  √( (-1)² + 0² )  =  √(1)  =  1

  Having no imaginary part, -1 is on the real (horizontal axis) -- having a negative real part, it's on the left semi-plane.
  Getting there requires a half a turn, so a flat angle -- so π. So  arg (-1)  =  π.
  
  Then:

    -1  =  |-1| exp ( j arg (-1) )  =  exp (jπ)

