  I've already secretly verified. If you don't like trigonometry, I hope I'm about to change your mind.

  Cosine starts at one. Compared to sine, it's a quarter-period early -- or three quarters late.
  So when sine reaches its first symmetry axis at π/2, cosine reaches its symmetry center.

  So the interval [π/2 , π] corresponds to sine positive and decreasing and cosine negative and decreasing.
  And [0 , π/2] has sine increasing and still positive and cosine positive, still decreasing.

  This means: to get the values excluded by our restriction on the maximal value of ν,
  all we need is to reverse cosine's sign.

  We don't have to examinate our poles to group them by conjugate pairs: we build the pairs ourselves.

