  We have poles. Let's make transfer functions.

  But first let's notice something.
  Something that's usually the introduction to a regular Butterworth chapter.

  //  A: unit-circle

  All our poles have the form:

    j cos (φ)  -  sin (φ)  =  j sin (φ + π/2)  -  cos (φ - π/2)  =  j sin (φ + π/2)  +  cos (φ -π/2 + π)
                           =  cos (φ + π/2)  +  j sin (φ + π/2)  =  cos (φ2)  +  j sin (φ2)
                           =  exp (jφ2)
  
  or the same with negative cosine, which, knowing that they're conjugates, gives:    exp (-jφ2)

  The absolute value is always 1. Which is the radius of the unit-circle.
  So all our poles are located on the left half of the unit-circle.
  (For the dark-energy filter, it would be the right half.)

  //  B: equal spacing

  We've found several poles inside a single complex sinusoid by making use of the trigonometric modulus.

                 μ - 2ν         2N - 1 - 2ν              π          π
    φ (ν)  =  π --------  =  π -------------  =  π  -  -----  -  ν ---
                   2N               2N                  2 Ν         N
  
  We're splitting π (half the unit-circle) in equal slices of  π/N  with an initial offset of  π/2N.
  Down to π/2 -- or, since we mirror cosine: down to 0 + π/2N.
  /*
    The range [0 , π] is the top half of the unit-circle.
    But once we convert sine in cosine and cosine in sine to reach the standard polar notation,
    the half-circle gets shifted a quarter-turn counter-clockwise (and the imaginary part switches sign).
    The range becomes [-π/2 , π/2]. Or rather:  [ -π/2 + π/2N  ,  π/2 - π/2N ]
  */

  These equal slices make our poles equally spaced.

  Notice that when N tends to infinity:

    π/N  and  π/2N  both tend to zero

    then the range  [ -π/2 + π/2N  ,  π/2 - π/2N ]  tends to  [-π/2 , π/2]
    which is the full left half of the unit-circle -- i.e. all the possible stable poles on the circle;
    and the space between poles tends to zero.

  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/cukzft6ntq

  //  A + B

  The poles of a Butterworth filter are equally spaced on the left half of the unit-circle.
  Their range and their proximity both grow with the order of the filter.

  I first met Butterworth filters through this "definition".
  It didn't make sense to me.
  That tends to happen when you put effect before cause.
  The equal spacing on the unit circle is a consequence of the initial concept. Not the other way around.
  Instead of:

    A Butterworth filter has the optimal pass-band and stop-band because its poles are this way.

  We've proven that:

    Its poles are this way because that's where you get if you seek optimal pass-band and stop-band.

