  Let's name stuff.

  We're going to build a filter of order N.
  It's a Butterworth filter, so its name will be B and its transfer function B (s').

  It will be decomposed in second-order filters and (if N is odd) an extra first-order filter.
  These filters will be called β_ν and their transfer functions β_ν (s').
  Where ν is a placeholder for each filter's index.

  The second-order filters β_ν will be obtained by multiplying conjugate poles p_ν and q_ν.

  Each pole p_ν or q_ν is built from an angle φ_ν. Such that:

    #  p_ν  =  +j cos (φ_ν)  -  sin (φ_ν)

    #  q_ν  =  -j cos (φ_ν)  -  sin (φ_ν)

  Ok, that's probably all we need for now.

  /*
    Let's address the special case: 1 pole.
    We've found that it's always -1.
  
    Let's solve:    -j cos (φ_ν)  -  sin (φ_ν)  =  -1
    Cosine first: we want cos (x) = 0.
  
      cos (x)  =  sin (x + π/2)  =  ± sin (x + π/2 ± π)    ;    sin (0) = 0
  
      =>    cos (x) = 0  <=>  x  =  π/2 mod π
  
    Ok, sine now: we want sin (x) = 1.
    Maximal value, so first quarter-period, so 2π/4 = π/2.
    
    Perfect, we don't need the modulus in cos.

    So:

      φ_ν = π/2    and    -j cos (φ_ν)  -  sin (φ_ν)  =  +j cos (φ_ν)  -  sin (φ_ν)  =  -1  =  p_ν  =  q_ν

    The formula works. We knew that.
    Plus if you've looked at the Desmos link in the previous section, you've literally seen this result.

    But it's worth noticing that if we rely blindly on our algorithm, we will generate the real single pole twice.
    If we automate the process not carefully, we could inadvertently add an extra 1-pole filter to our cascade.

    Oh by the way:
    cascade is a standard term for what we're currently doing: stacking filters in series to build a bigger filter;
    or dividing a big filter into smaller ones.
    Attested advantage: polynomials of order greater than 2 are a pain to discriminate (I've tried of course).
    Rumour: I personally don't understand why and therefore don't consider it undoubtedly true,
    but digital filters of higher order seem to be less prone to denormal noise when split this way.
    (My personal, maybe wrong, hypothesis: the z^-N previous states are as negligible as N is high if the filter is stable --
     -- N = 2 is often as low as we can get without introducing imaginary gains... ?
  */


  //  β_ν^-1 (s')

  Let's temporarily simplify our notation de-crowd information:

    β_ν^-1 (s')  =  (s' - p_ν) (s' - q_ν)    <----    1/β  =  (s' - p)(s' - q)

    cos (φ_ν)  <----  cos

    sin (φ_ν)  <----  sin

  We'll easily reverse the these substitutions once our expression is clean enough.
  Also: N is even until further notice.

    p  =  +j cos  -  sin    ;    q  =  -j cos  -  sin    ;    1/β  =  (s' - p)(s' - q)

    =>    1/β  =  ( s'  -  j cos  +  sin ) ( s'  +  j cos  +  sin )

               =  s'²  +  j cos s'  +  sin s'  -  j cos s'  +  cos²  -  j cos sin  +  sin s'  +  j cos sin  +  sin²

  Ok, let's group simplifying terms before removing them:

    1/β  =  s'²  +  [ sin s'  +  sin s' ]  +  [ cos²  +  sin² ]  +  [ j cos s'  -  j cos s' ]  +  [ j cos sin  -  j cos sin ]

  The two last pairs of brackets both sum down to zero, we can forget them.
  The first pair of brackets adds two identical terms: it can be simplified with a factor 2.

  And, of course, the second pair of brackets is self-explanatory because, obviously, everybody remembers their Pythagoras...

  /*
    Just in case:

      adjacent² + opposite²  =  hypotenuse²

      cos  =  adjacent / hypotenuse    ;    sin  =  opposite / hypotenuse

                        adjacent²         opposite²        adjacent² + opposite²       hypotenuse²
      cos² + sin²  =  -------------  +  -------------  =  -----------------------  =  -------------  =  1
                       hypotenuse²       hypotenuse²            hypotenuse²            hypotenuse²

    Obviously!
    This demonstration goes even quicker if you assume hypotenuse = 1.
    But I tend to consider that zero prior assumption makes the result feel more "always true".
  */

  Ok, so:
                                                                       1
