  Ok. If N is even, we multiply all second-order transfer functions β_ν (s) with H (s). That's it.

  How do we write it as a formula without clumsy conditional branching?

  //  Détour

  If N is even,  N mod 2  =  0    ;    if N is odd,  N mod 2  = 1

  Basic exponentiation rule:  x^0 = 1  and  x^1 = x.

  So:  x^(N mod 2)  =  [ 1 if N is even  ||  x if N is odd ]

  So:  H (s)^(N mod 2)  =  [ 1 if N is even  ||  H (s) if N is odd ]

  Let's define:

    o  =  N mod 2  ("o" as in "o"dd...)    and    Π (s) the product of all second-order instances.

  Then, if N is even:

    H (s)^o Π (s)  =  Π (s)  =  B (s)

  and, if N is odd:

    H (s)^o Π (s)  =  H (s) Π (s)  =  B (s)

  Almost smells like success.

  Except one last nit pick: what if N = 1 ?
  Then we don't want Π (s).
  An exponent switching between zero and one should do again:  N = 1  -->  Π (s)^0    ;    N > 1  -->  Π (s)^1.
  Ok, got it:
    
    N  =  1    <=>    N - 1  =  0    ;    N  >  1    <=>    N - 1  >  0

  Let's call our exponent α. Then we need α = 0 when N = 1 and α = 1 when N > 1.
  So α should always pick the smallest value between 1 and N - 1. This way it will always be 1 except when N - 1 = 0.
  This function is called the min function: input two numbers, output the smallest of the two.

    https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/algorithm/min        //  Not Wikipedia. I've noticed, thanks. Sue me.

  What if the two inputs are equal? ... Well it won't create a third number. So N = 2 will compare 1 and 1, and output 1. Alles gut.

    α  =  min (1 , N - 1)    ;    B (s)  =  H (s)^o Π (s)^α

  How about we write the complete algorithm in one single formula with all terms fully developed, just once? For fun?

