  //  Fun fact
  Formula (1) starts with negative angles and moves upward.
  Formula (2) starts with positive angles and moves downward.
  Then we flip the signs, so North-East goes South-West and South-East goes North-West.
  So formula (1) starts in the top-left quadrant and goes downward:
  and formula (2) starts in the bottom-left quadrant and goes upward.
  This means that at each equal value of n, method (1) and method (2) compute a conjugate pair.
  If N is odd they end up joining at the real pole.
  Which means that  p (n)  and  p (N - 1 - n)  are always a conjugate pair with both formulae.
  ( E.g.:  N = 5    =>    arg (p (2))  =  π (1 - 5 + 4)/10  =  0        &&        arg (p (5 - 1 - 2))  =  π (1 - 5 + 10 - 2 - 4)/10  =  0 )

  "Fun fact" and not "useful fact" because using two formulae in parallel is indeed fun, but not optimal as an algorithm.
  A better algorithm would be: choose one formula, then either (a) run the full loop, or (b) stop half-way and reverse all imaginary parts.
  I like (a) better, but to be very practical, the dev's formula is probably the most useful.
  Formulae (1) and (2) serve mathematical elegance -- not necessarily the engineer's first choice.
  Still, their simplicity makes them safe. And having them available could help fix buggy designs.


